      "latency_ms": 3184.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 692,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is arguably the most important substance for life on Earth. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and find in rivers and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F), though it also evaporates into the air at lower temperatures.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of the way its electrical charge is distributed (it is a polar molecule), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it transports vital nutrients in our bodies and minerals in the soil.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in a glass, and crucially, why lakes freeze from the top down rather than the bottom up, allowing aquatic life to survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Abundance:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite how much of it there is, about **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly recycled through a closed loop known as the hydrologic cycle: it evaporates from the oceans into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows back into the oceans via rivers.\n\n### 4. Why Water Matters for Life\nBiologically, water is the medium of life. \n*   The human body is made up of about **60% water**. \n*   Cells are mostly water; it provides the watery environment (cytoplasm) in which all the cellular machinery of life operates.\n*   Without water, chemical reactions necessary for life (like metabolism and digestion) cannot take place. \n\nIn summary, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that acts as the lifeblood of our planet."
